00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.280
Hello Internet!

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.920
Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.000
Now, let me make one thing clear up front: "Us" is a great movie,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.460
but it's also a hard movie.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.800
I think everyone was really  excited to see

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.900
what Jordan Peele's follow up to Get Out would be,

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.580
and Us doesn't disappoint

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:15.020
It's just different than the horror movie that I think a lot of people were expecting, myself included.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.460
Whereas "Get Out" is pretty self-explanatory in its themes about race,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.340
"Us" plays with a lot of big ideas that don't immediately seem to fit together.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.300
There are themes of religion, race, duality, free will,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.360
all told through the story of underground mazes, cloning experiments, nationwide hand-holding,

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:33.880
the smooth flow of "Loonies"...

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.800
"What does I got five on it mean?"

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.100
"It's about drugs"

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.500
... and rabbits. A whole lot of rabbits.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.100
Now, when I say it's a hard movie,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.380
I mean that it's one that requires a lot of thought,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.260
and even some research to really appreciate and understand.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:51.180
And because it has this big twist and really explanatory monologue at the very end,

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.400
I think a lot of people walk out of this assuming that they got it

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.220
just because they got the surface level information about a girl who was swapped with her soulless double,

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.580
but there is so much more here.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:06.400
The horror of this movie isn't The Tethered and their aggressive use of scissors.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.020
It's not even how creepy, mildly racist, fun houses on the strands of tourist beaches

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.820
lead to underground testing facilities.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.440
No,  'Us' is really about the failure of the American Dream.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.520
What you can see throughout the movie are glimpses of the perfect American life,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.900
where all the characters seem to be doing great,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:29.480
but all of these moments are actually hollow, meaningless, or just weirdly miss the mark in some way.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.600
And by the end, it's not the clones that are killing us, it's America.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.800
It's us- the U.S.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.000
Let's look at the facts.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.540
What first clued me in that this is the direction the film was headed was a really odd line,

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.340
during the first encounter with The Tethered.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.320
The doppelgangers have broken into the lake house of the Wilsons,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.040
and have everyone seated for a good old story time

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.740
but when the family's father, Gabe, asks The Tethered, "Who are you?",

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.740
The response is one that catches everyone off guard,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.540
both in the movie, and out in the theater,

00:02:58.540 --> 00:02:59.980
Not that these are twins,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.420
not that they're The Tethered,

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.700
not even that they're shadows, or a weird government science experiment gone wrong,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.300
No, the response is

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.300
"We're Americans"

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.440
We're Americans.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.860
It's weird, right?

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.140
And not like random weird,

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.540
This line is clearly weird with a purpose.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.020
Any time a character sits down the villain and says, "What are you?"

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.140
You know that the response is gonna be important.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.560
And it got me thinking.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.720
And also got me buying another ticket to watch it from the beginning with this new context.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.110
And looking all the way through the film from start to finish,

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:33.300
it becomes clear that we see the theme of America and the American Dream

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.420
play an oddly important role throughout all the events of the film.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.520
The opening seconds of the movie are spent telling us some random facts about underground tunnels.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:47.640
Apparently miles of tunnels crisscross underneath the United States that are now abandoned, empty and forgotten.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.040
Now, why tell us this?

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.900
Especially as the very first thing we see as a part of this movie?

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.749
I mean, sure, the ending takes place in one of those tunnels,

00:03:55.749 --> 00:04:00.960
but that seems like overkill to start the movie off on something that barely pays off,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.400
and no one would really question if it wasn't there.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.460
Instead, I think the reason why is that it sets up the American Dream theme immediately.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:13.620
America, the country, is often considered superficial figuratively,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.600
but also, America, the country is superficial literally.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.320
There is nothing under the surface.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.340
It's empty.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:22.140
It's hollow.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.680
All America is, is on the surface,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.870
and underneath are just a bunch of abandoned tunnels filled with nothing.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:30.020
You think that's a stretch?

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.880
Honestly, I wouldn't blame you, it seems like a pretty big leap to make,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.400
but let's look at another key element of the movie: the Hands Across America campaign.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.080
Less than two minutes into the film, we see a commercial for this thing,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.820
and then it's the final shot of the movie,

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.080
with thousands of Tethered joining hands once they kill off their surface-dwelling counterpart

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.700
You might not be familiar with it, but Hands Across America took place in 1986,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.980
and involved 15 minutes of people holding hands across the country

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.020
to raise money to "cure homelessness and hunger".

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.040
It happened at the height of the Reagan era, which was a time marked by a lot of national growth

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.200
and an 80's-tastic feeling of positivity on one hand,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:08.960
but also blind unchecked capitalism and business wheeling and dealing on the other,

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.080
and nothing shows this better than Hands Across America.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:18.540
The event itself managed to raise a massive amount of money: 32 million dollars.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.300
Which is great, especially when it's back in the 1980's,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:28.480
until you consider that the company that ran this thing paid out 17 million of those dollars to pay its own bills,

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:36.480
and when it comes to charitable events, any "administration fees" over 20% are considered to be pretty darn egregious,

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.680
so the homeless saw much less of that total amount than it initially seemed.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.860
So already in this opening moment, we see what appears to be a great moment of

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.520
"Yeah! America uniting for change!"

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.060
and it's this grand gesture that looks awesome on the outside, is emotionally moving,

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.980
but is already questionable what's under the surface.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:01.220
It looked great on the surface, but when it came to actual substance, it, once again, is empty.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.360
But okay, we need to move on from the opening scenes to the actual movie.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.200
Fast forward to the present day version of our protagonist Adelaide and her family,

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.620
and you see they are full of clichés of the perfect American life...

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:15.540
...or, more accurately, almost the perfect American life.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.820
Jordan Peele has chosen to include a bunch of symbols that show the failure or letdown of the American Dream:

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.860
Throughout the movie, Gabe wears a Howard University sweatshirt,

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.120
which, if you aren't familiar, is a historically black college,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.480
along with other high-end universities like Hampton and the Tuskegee Institute.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.860
These schools are all part of the black Ivy League,

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:39.820
a group of prestigious schools that were specifically available to African Americans back when segregation was still a thing.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:46.000
So, on one hand, they represent what every American dreams of: going to a good college, the Ivy League,

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:51.160
but the other hand is that it's a group of schools that specifically came out of segregation.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:55.680
A dark spot on America's past, and part of a long history of racial inequality.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.580
For a period of time, these schools were also considered to be the schools that black students could go to,

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:07.020
that would qualify them for careers that were traditionally considered "beyond the reach" of African Americans at the time;

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.700
jobs only available to upper-middle class white people.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.580
Again, the movie highlights this desire we all have to go to an amazing school like Howard,

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.080
but the full context of historically black colleges

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.500
also refers to a time when black students were considered second-class.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:23.440
It's the American Dream again, but twisted, corrupted.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.200
And since we're talking about Gabe, let's also talk about Gabe's boat.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.840
Throughout the movie, we see a lot of characters that have a lot of really nice stuff.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.120
Having nice stuff is something we all want, and something people are currently obsessed with,

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.020
from flex culture to Yeezys.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.080
But here, we see that it's not quite working the way that everyone hopes.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:42.640
If you're a middle-class person in the US,

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:47.000
there's a good chance that your dad dreamed of buying a boat at some point or another.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.749
It is the ultimate symbol of expendable income:

00:07:49.749 --> 00:07:55.440
something purely for relaxation that costs a lot to maintain and that you use, like, once a year.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.960
And here Gabe is, achieving the dream, checking off the "I'm on a boat" box,

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.940
but, the boat doesn't work.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.980
It turns off at random, it pulls to the left.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.820
"It veers a little to the left, so you gotta hold on to it,

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.620
It's the American Dream in name, but in substance it's empty.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:15.520
Not only is the boat not all it's cracked up to be, it's not just the boat,

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.680
you also have to have all the stuff that goes with it.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:23.100
It's never enough to have what you have, you always need to have more.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:25.360
The boat is certainly the most prominent example,

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:28.620
but you also see it when Gabe talks about the Tylers' car,

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.620
"You saw their new car, right?"

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:31.920
And their backup generator.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.740
The American dream isn't a destination.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.660
It's just constantly trying to get more than you already have,

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:40.840
and constantly being dissatisfied by what you do have.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:46.720
Speaking of all that stuff, after seeing the Tylers literally die all over their beautiful house,

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.260
Gabe still sees all the stuff as the thing that's going to protect him.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:55.600
"We got everything we need here; food, water, backup generator."

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:01.860
Materialism is the shield, it's the solution because it's always the solution in America.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.340
Rewind back to the first home invasion scene:

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.200
Gabe's tactic, again, is the stuff,

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.560
to keep offering The Tethered more material goods as the thing to make them go away,

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:16.760
a direct nod to the perceived power of capitalism in making your problems go away.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:18.340
"We don't have anything here."

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.500
"This is our summer home."

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.240
Come on man, this is not the time for that sort of flex!

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.960
Gabe follows that up by offering the boat, to empty his bank account.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:32.240
The irony here is that all that stuff isn't protecting anyone, but it is killing everyone.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.480
Did you notice that throughout the movie, even though scissors are the main weapon,

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.880
they rarely are the thing that kills anyone?

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:39.080
At least, onscreen.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:42.100
After Tethered Adelaide notes that "we're Americans",

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.080
people aren't using knives and guns.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:47.720
They're using symbols of the American upper middle class.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:52.320
During the opening attack, Gabe wields the great American pastime of a baseball bat.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:55.740
Death #1, Gabe kills Abraham with the new boat.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:00.500
Zora kills one Tethered Tyler twin and almost the other with a golf putter.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:04.780
Death #3, Gabe kills Tethered Josh with the flare gun from the boat.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:08.260
The Amazon Alexa fails to call the police, leading to more death,

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.060
Jason kills Tethered Kitty with a mounted rock,

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:11.780
"They're minerals!"

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:13.640
"I've got some geodes coming that are very delicate!"

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:17.740
and Death #5, Zora kills Umbrae with a high-end white Land Rover.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:22.420
These deaths literally and figuratively beat the characters over the head,

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:25.620
with the idea that their stuff isn't doing good in the world,

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:26.720
it's a weapon.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:30.820
It also shows that at the end of the day, we're all dying to this materialism,

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:34.220
and slowly being killed by our desire for more stuff.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:37.920
Even Tethered Kitty and Josh can't resist the pull of capitalism:

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:40.480
they put on the bathrobe, they put on makeup,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.840
they even try a little DIY plastic surgery in pursuit of this beautiful life.

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.040
that their counterparts, the real surface Tylers had.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.740
They think that it'll be the thing that makes them happy, that gives them substance,

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.680
it's all the soft things that they grew up wanting but never had.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.440
and the irony is, it's not gonna make them happy,

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.480
because this is America.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.400
She puts on the makeup, it makes her happy for a second,

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:07.660
but then she starts seeing imperfections and immediately starts slicing into her face.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:11.620
You continue to see how futile this American life is when the families hit the beach,

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.700
and everything ain't so perfect.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:17.000
Gabe and Josh spend their entire time there comparing stuff,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.580
showing very clearly that even if you're doing well,

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:22.980
in America, you'll still always be comparing it to the people around you,

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.740
and you're not doing good enough.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.980
Even those who are successful aren't happy.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:32.800
There's Kitty, the perfect suburban mom of two who is immediately self-medicating with alcohol...

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.420
"Gotta give my wife her medicine."

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:38.260
...to numb her from the marriage to a guy who cares more about his stuff than her,

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:41.880
who doesn't take her fear seriously when she hears noises outside their house.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:43.640
"I think about murdering him sometimes."

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:45.940
She might have a nice life, but it's not good enough,

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:47.720
which is why she's been getting plastic surgery,

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:49.360
"Did you get something done?"

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.640
"Just like an itsy bitsy little thing."

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:54.320
and wishes she'd become an actress, instead of having kids.

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.020
"I think I could've been a movie star."

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:57.000
"I went to Stella Adler."

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.880
This is the real picture of the American Dream.

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:02.540
It isn't about having everything you want and being happy,

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:05.700
It's about having the appearance of having everything you want,

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:08.880
but not ever actually being satisfied with any of it.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:11.580
All the stuff, the boats, the silicone noses.

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:12.840
They're all empty.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:16.360
Maybe they used to have meaning, but now, they're just superficial symbols.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:21.440
For proof that this is exactly what Jordan Peele wanted, look no further than the Tyler twins.

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.780
You see the one who's wearing a "Black Flag" t-shirt?

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:25.720
I thought it was a weird detail,

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:29.840
especially when you consider that the carnival worker at the beginning of the movie is wearing one too.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.500
Clearly, it's meant to mean something.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:35.880
Now, he's wearing it in 1986, when the band "Black Flag" was at their peak,

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.340
and were known for founding the West Coast punk rock counterculture revolution.

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.139
They were considered really subversive in their day.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:47.420
But then you fast forward to now, when Lindsey Tyler's wearing it here,

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:49.660
and it's not because she's hardcore or subversive.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:52.700
I mean, she's a blonde teen who's doing gymnastics at the beach.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:54.880
She's not going counter to anything.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:58.520
She's actually the peak of American cultural conformity,

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:01.720
where this edgy idea of a renegade band called Black Flag

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:05.320
has been appropriated into American tween materialism.

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:11.879
I mean, she is literally a twin who is wearing something that stands for lack of conformity!

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:13.800
You don't get more on the nose than that.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:15.920
Personally, it's my favorite symbol in the entire film.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:16.820
I think it's brilliant.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:17.740
She's a twin!

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:19.820
She's literally a copy of someone else!!

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:24.820
And finally, in the closing sequence, we get our last and maybe most damning piece of evidence,

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:26.420
for this commentary on America:

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:30.580
we finally get the showdown between Adelaide and her Tethered in the tunnels,

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:33.220
and it's finally revealed- one more spoiler alert- that

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:38.040
Tethered Adelaide is actually the one that we've been hoping to survive the entire time.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:42.380
They switched as little kids, and we've been seeing the tethered version the entire film!

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.380
Now, I've seen a lot of viewers actually complain about this twist,

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:48.200
citing the fact that it would have been more fun to leave it ambiguous,

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:50.380
and not explicitly tell us that they'd been switched.

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:53.600
It wasn't my favorite twist either, but that's the thing:

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:57.180
for this theory to work, you have to know it explicitly.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:59.680
The point isn't that there's this big twist and,

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.820
"Whoa! Adelaide isn't who she thought she was!"

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:04.100
and it completely changes everything we saw before,

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:08.980
the whole point is that for the entire movie, we couldn't tell the difference.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:11.480
And neither could anyone else around her.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:12.740
I mean, think about that.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:17.400
Our society is so shallow and vapid and surface-level,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:21.880
that an entire person got replaced by a copy, a clone,

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:28.520
a science experiment that the movie explicitly tells us has no soul, and no one skipped a beat.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:31.780
No one could tell the difference.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:37.160
As long as it looked the same on the outside, it didn't matter what was on the inside.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:39.920
Like Adelaide says at the end of the movie to her son Jason,

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.840
"It's gonna be just like it was before."

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:43.180
Nothing is gonna change.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:45.720
We're going to keep on pursuing this empty life.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:50.280
It doesn't matter who was a soulless tethered person and who was the original.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:54.320
And yeah, Jason knows that his mom is really the tethered version of Adelaide,

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.180
but do we see him making a big deal out of it?

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:57.720
No, just the opposite.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.520
He ignores it, accepts it.

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:02.960
He puts on his mask, his facade, and he carries on.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:05.780
We pan across suburbia, and everyone is dead.

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:07.760
White, black, doesn't matter.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.400
The American Dream didn't save anyone.

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:13.380
We close on a shot of Hands Across America.

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:15.760
At the end of the day, you may or may not agree with that message.

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:17.440
You may have a boat and think it's awesome.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:19.700
Heck, I think a boat sounds amazing.

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:22.440
But this is what I think Jordan Peele was trying to say,

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:25.820
with all of those crazy mixed-up symbols used throughout the movie.

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:29.260
I'm writing this the day after the movie came out, by the way,

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:33.600
after having watched it 3 times in the theater, so maybe everyone already knows all this?

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:35.000
Maybe it's all been picked to death, I don't know.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.660
These videos take way too long to make, so forgive me if all this stuff has been covered to death.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:41.240
I just thought it was an interesting movie to discuss,

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:46.260
one that seems to explain a lot, but still leaves some of the biggest mysteries for us to discover on our own.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.600
Those are some of the most exciting movies to do episodes on,

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.700
because it means never-ending theories!

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:53.280
Film Theories!

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:53.960
Aaaaannndddd....

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:56.400
Oh, did you notice the recurring themes of red, white, and blue?

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:00.420
Or how Adelaide starts white, but then slowly becomes more red as the movie goes on?

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:02.140
The fake spider and the real spider?

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:05.600
Or how rabbits are completely useless creatures with no real purpose?

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.760
Aargh! I just really like this movie and want to keep talking about it, okay?

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.900
It's worth seeing so you can come up with your own theories.

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:12.380
You know what is also worth doing?

